**CodeQL backlog: 2,353 open alerts — 1 critical, 35 high, 57 medium.** Of the 36 high/critical,
**11 are real, 25 are false positives.** The 25 are NOT yet dismissed — deliberately. Dismissing a
security alert on an un-reverified analysis is the failure this session spent its effort avoiding.

**The two riskiest dismissals (#11249, #11244) WERE spot-verified — both are genuine pass-through
sinks, safe to dismiss.** But verifying them found #2030: CodeQL flagged the harmless generic
`logger.error` at `base_agent.py:744` and did NOT flag lines 727/735 two functions up, which
unconditionally render whole agent I/O dicts at INFO. **Dismiss #11244 only alongside the #2030
fix** — otherwise the file gets recorded as reviewed-and-clean while the real leak stands. This is
the session's theme in miniature: the instrument reports on what its queries model, not on what is
risky. The remaining 23 dismissals still need the same treatment before any bulk action.

## THE finding: five issues had wrong SPECIFICATIONS, not wrong code

A diligent session implements each faithfully and closes it wrongly. Verify before implementing.

- **#2004** — its own prescribed fix (route through `mask_error_text`) **provably does not redact**.
  That helper handles URL userinfo and query params; the threat is a CLI flag `--token=`. No
